WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.170
- Welcome back to the start
of the afternoon session.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.410
I'm going to do a brief introduction

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:17.480
and then pass it off to our
moderator, Beverly Gage.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.380
As you can see from the
description in your program,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.960
this panel centers on the societal context

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.460
in which co-education occurred,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.860
including society's
expectations for women,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.000
the expectations women had for themselves,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.550
and the culture of the all male
institution we were joining.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.230
As you well know, many things that strike

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.650
a younger audience is odd
about how Yale welcomed

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.780
the first women undergraduates.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.930
From the facilities, to the traditions,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.080
to the certain limitations
on where, when or what

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.790
a woman could do or be,

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.850
were very much standard thinking

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.883
in the world outside of Yale.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.410
So, one really can't make
sense of Yale's transition

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.570
to co-education without putting it

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.240
in the appropriate context.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.030
We hope this panel will
provide a thoughtful discussion

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.560
of those years before Yale went co-ed,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.000
and when the women first came to campus.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.390
And will it assess what
all of that meant for women

00:01:24.611 --> 00:01:27.690
in the US in the late 1960s.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.630
Many of our mothers
hoped we would not follow

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.330
in their footsteps.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.120
And instead wished for
us the opportunities

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.283
they were denied.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.340
So, I'm going to turn it over to Beverly,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.711
but first give you a little intro on her.

00:01:44.711 --> 00:01:46.650
As you can see in your program, too,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.240
the full bios are listed there,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.610
so we're only gonna list
a few of the highlights.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.160
Beverly Gage, class of '94,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.650
is professor of 20th
century American history,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.810
and director of the Brady Johnson
Program in grand strategy.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.930
Her courses focus on American politics,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.970
political thought, social movements,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.760
and governments broadly conceived.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.340
In addition to her Yale
BA, she holds a PhD

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.120
in history from Columbia.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.724
So please welcome Beverly Gage.

00:02:19.724 --> 00:02:24.057
(audience and panelists applauding)

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.050
- Well thank you all.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.800
Are the microphones in better shape?

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.030
First and most important question, okay.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.970
That's wonderful to hear.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.230
Thank you all, first
of all for being here,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.360
and thank you to the
organizers Eve Rice especially,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.801
who did so much work to
put this panel together.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:44.801
(audience applauds)

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.420
And I also want to say
a heartfelt thank you

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.420
to all of you on behave of the
slightly younger generations

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.460
of women Yale students, who
followed in your footsteps,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.270
who would not have been
able to have the experiences

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:07.270
that we had without your
bravery, hard work, adventurism.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.110
And also on behalf of the
women faculty here at Yale.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.240
I was at this morning's
session and I appreciated

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.610
the conversation and
advocacy on our behalf.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.440
I should say, looking out at this audience

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.200
it's kind of a thrill to be up here during

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.360
the Yale reunions in May.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.730
I was a speaker at the
class of 1969 reunion,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.511
who looked very different from this.

00:03:33.511 --> 00:03:34.344
(everyone laughs)

00:03:34.344 --> 00:03:37.410
So this is a real triumph,
it's really remarkable

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.030
to sit up here and see
all of you out there.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.220
It's just wonderful that
this event is happening.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.030
Our task up here for this
panel is to draw back

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:51.570
a little bit from Yale
centric discussions,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.420
and think about how all
of your experiences.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.920
And the experiences of the institution

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.310
and the broader community of New Haven,

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:04.370
fit into some larger political, social,

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.280
cultural, intellectual
dynamics that were underway

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.390
during the late 1960s and early 1970s.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:16.000
As a history professor I
teach a class in 20th century

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.590
American history here, so we
cover a lot of this ground.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.020
I spend a lot of time
teaching this as history

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.330
to the next generation
(audience laughs)

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.650
of students who I should
note, somewhat disturbingly

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.640
even for me, who are now
born in the 21st century.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.190
So this year's freshman and sophomores

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.380
were all 21st century babies.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.820
So all of the 20th century,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.860
including my own
undergraduate experiences,

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:45.310
of course yours is now a very much part

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.330
of history for them.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.940
And, when we talk about
history I actually often

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:54.210
point back to this moment in
the late '60s and early '70s

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.630
when students come to me with the question

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.880
of whether or not we've
lived through anything like

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.830
what we're living through today.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.190
But in particular, whether we
have ever lived in a society

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:10.190
that was as divided, and as
full of political passion as

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.200
the world into which they are entering.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.150
And of course there are
lots of differences between

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:18.100
this moment and 50 years ago,

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.040
but I think there are
some really interesting

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.720
and telling similarities, too.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.650
And so that's part of our challenge today

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.550
is to talk about what
some of those might be.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.190
Just to refresh everyone's memory,

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.390
1969 through the early 1970s,

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:36.210
so Richard Nixon had just
been elected president.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.540
We were at really the
peak of the Vietnam War

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.070
as well as the controversies
over the Vietnam War.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.480
The Civil Rights Movement
had been underway

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.030
for well over a decade
in its formal sense.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.970
And in many ways, by the
late 1960s we were entering

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.850
a moment of really new
radical consciousness,

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.680
for the anti-war movement,
for the Civil Rights Movement,

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.530
for many other identity movements

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.300
that were beginning to emerge.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.100
One of the most interesting
things that I've been thinking

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:11.840
about in 1969, was sort of
the parallel story to that,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.160
which is a book that was
written by a man named

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.527
Keven Philips in 1969 titled,

00:06:16.527 --> 00:06:19.097
"The Emerging Republican Majority."

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.700
It was sort of laying out what he saw,

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.910
despite all of this radicalization,

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:28.280
despite all of this energy
that seemed to be happening

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.770
on the left and within liberal circles.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:35.310
He foresaw a radical
reordering of politics on

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.760
the American right, as well.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.840
And that's become
increasingly for historians,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.197
a big theme of the 1960s and 1970s.

00:06:42.197 --> 00:06:45.580
And of course for people
who were teenagers

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.640
and in their early 20s, these
were both thrilling years.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:53.100
And I would imagine terrifying
years in lots of ways.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.620
1968 saw the assassinations
of Robert F. Kennedy

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.660
and Martin Luther King.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:02.540
You were seeing increasing
violence in many American cities,

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:06.440
and of course in 1970, you
really saw dramatic ruptures

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.640
on many campuses in the United States.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.010
I'm sure everyone in
this room has some memory

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:15.580
of Mayday, 1970, and that might be one of

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.920
the touchstone events we'd
like to talk about here.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.080
But of course, 1970 was the year of

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:22.690
the Kent State shootings,

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.380
the shootings of student protestors

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.610
at Jackson State in Mississippi as well.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:29.420
So, if we had to boil it down,

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.440
I think we might think about the period

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.950
in which you all arrived at Yale,

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.520
as a period of enormous
promise and change.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.080
A period of pretty serious instability,

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.740
and a divisiveness, as well.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:47.700
And so, we want to think
about the experiences

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.640
of everyone in this room,

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.743
but particularly our distinguished
panelists in that theme.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:54.850
So, to turn to our panelists,

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.660
I'm gonna briefly introduce
each of them at turn.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.840
We'll have a conversation
up here for about 45 minutes

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.470
to an hour, and then as
with the panel this morning,

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:07.130
we will open it up for
questions and answers.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:12.130
I suppose we can go from that
end of the table to this one.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.380
We'll start with Congressman
Sheila Jackson Lee,

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.480
who is a 1972 graduate--

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:19.813
- Don't start with me.
- Don't start with you.

00:08:19.813 --> 00:08:21.030
- I'm yielding to my sisters.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.850
- All right, she's busy.
(everyone laughs)

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:24.870
I'm gonna introduce you in that order,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.370
then we'll start somewhere else.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.510
A 1972 graduate of Yale
College, she is in her 11th term

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.300
in the United States
House of Representatives

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:37.037
representing--
(everyone applauds)

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.980
Representing the 18th
Congressional District of Texas,

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.930
which is centered around Houston.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.960
You might know her if
you're an avid CSPAN fan,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.240
but you might also see her
very frequently speaking out

00:08:54.525 --> 00:08:55.810
in the name of truth and justice

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.130
on many television programs and elsewhere.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.060
One of our most influential

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:04.520
and really prolific legislators today.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.660
Next we have Dr. Laura Wexler,

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.110
who is my colleague here at Yale,

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.760
professor of Women's, Gender,
and Sexuality Studies,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.000
and American Studies.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.940
She is an expert on American
culture in the present

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.080
and in the past particularly
on women's culture,

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.270
women's history, feminism,
as well as photography,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.680
film, media studies, ethnicity,

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.540
race and migration, public humanities.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.310
Professor Wexler is one of those of people

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.500
who seems to have her
fingers in many, many pies

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.630
here at Yale, which is
wonderful for all of us.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:42.053
Also here at Yale, I'd like
to introduce Linda Greenhouse,

00:09:44.903 --> 00:09:48.210
who's a 1978 graduate of Yale Law School.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.830
And since 2009 has been here

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.980
as the Joseph Goldstein lecturer in law.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.570
She spent 40 years plus
at the New York Times,

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:01.100
reporting on the law and
on the Supreme Court.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.920
She received a Pulitzer
Prize for her work there.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:07.810
She is the author of many books,
including a recent memoir,

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:11.400
which might be worth checking out

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.410
for all the personal details in there.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.830
And I'm sorry to say that
she has also been a member

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.303
of the Harvard University
Board of Overseers.

00:10:19.303 --> 00:10:20.136
(audience and panelists chuckling)

00:10:20.136 --> 00:10:22.157
That's the one blot
(crosstalk and laughter).

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:28.733
Next we have Dr. Virginia Dominguez.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:33.820
Dr. Dominguez is a 1973
graduate of Yale College,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.630
and a 1979 PhD from the

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.030
Graduate School of Arts and Sciences.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.760
She is the Edward William
&amp; Jane Marr Gutgsell

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.830
professor of Anthropology at
the University of Illinois

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.720
at Urbana-Champaign.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.370
She is an expert on

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.030
the United States in a global
context in thinking about

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.470
how we might tell American
history and other histories

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:59.610
through a lens that is not simply centered

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.100
in the United States.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.530
But looks much more
broadly at what the rest of

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:05.390
the world thinks of the United States.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:09.490
And how Americans might
think about the world.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:12.007
She's the author of many
books, including most recently,

00:11:12.007 --> 00:11:14.880
"America Observed On an International

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.460
Anthropology of the United States."

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.140
And finally we have Dr. Nancy Vickers,

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.540
who is a 1976 PhD from the

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:26.130
Graduate School of Arts and Sciences here.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.710
She was the seventh president
of Bryn Mawr College.

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:33.040
I think must have many
reflections on co-education

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.990
and its promise and perils.

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:37.130
While she was here at Yale,

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.230
she was for two years a member of

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.290
the University Committee
on Co-education at Yale.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:46.110
She's also a distinguished
scholar of literature

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:49.317
and of language, she's
written many books as well,

00:11:49.317 --> 00:11:51.900
"A New History of French
Literature," among them.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.092
So please join me in
welcoming these panelists.

00:11:55.092 --> 00:11:58.175
(audience applauds)

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.870
So I thought we might start
out with this question

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:11.780
of how much, when you were here at Yale,

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:14.560
you were thinking about
this broader scope.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.030
When historians think back about the past,

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:19.630
we tend to have this very high level view.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:22.230
We think about Richard
Nixon and the Vietnam War

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:23.490
and the Civil Rights Movement.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.500
And of course, those things intersect

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.400
with people's lived experiences.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.670
But to some degree, they
don't intersect with them

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:34.440
at all in some cases,
particularly 18 year olds.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:38.020
And of course up here, we want
to talk a little bit about

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.680
where we were in terms of
feminist consciousness,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.360
in terms of women's
consciousness at that moment.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:49.170
So, maybe Dr. Dominguez, do
you want to start us off?

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.370
Since you have both the global scale

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.523
of thinking about this and
the personal experience.

00:12:54.523 --> 00:12:55.600
- I will do my best.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:58.320
Since I was here this
morning and the microphones

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.090
were not working, let me do what you did.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.004
Can you hear me?

00:13:01.004 --> 00:13:02.421
- [Audience] Yes.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.335
- I'm probably most unusual
here and not neccessarily

00:13:07.335 --> 00:13:10.220
for what you said about us.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.630
But, I really didn't live in the US

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:14.963
for the four years prior to starting.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:22.220
Some of the things I
remember were pressure.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:25.563
I desperately tried not
to flunk out of Yale.

00:13:28.683 --> 00:13:31.410
I remember a lot of my
friends and colleagues

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.810
going on marches in Washington,

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.050
'cause I remember the Vietnam War well.

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:39.430
But, I was actually not
living in the US at the time.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:41.717
I went to high school, I think
at least some of you know

00:13:41.717 --> 00:13:44.330
I went to high school
in (speaks foreign word)

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:48.447
to an all girls Catholic
school. (chuckles)

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.870
What I remember was I
actually watched the landing

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:59.150
on the moon in Guadalajara,
Mexico with my mother on TV.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:01.433
I mean I remember that vividly.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:04.640
I don't remember various other things.

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.560
I do remember becoming an Agnostic.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.750
I said I went to a Catholic girls school.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:12.823
I remember becoming an
Agnostic when I was about 14.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.650
The principal of the
school actually loved that.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.802
She then went on to
become a union organizer,

00:14:21.802 --> 00:14:23.310
(audience laughs)
and social worker

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.200
after I graduated, so you
know I was sort of following

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.040
in her footsteps, what little did I know?

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:28.873
Was I a feminist?

00:14:28.873 --> 00:14:29.706
I don't know.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.770
I really don't know, I cannot remember.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.500
Did I become a feminist while I was here?

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:36.520
Yes.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:38.050
Was I a feminist beforehand?

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:40.360
I don't know, but it never occurred to me

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.940
that frankly that Yale, and/or Princeton

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.080
would take so long to accept women,

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:47.513
because it made no sense.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:53.548
I do remember Nixon. (chuckles)

00:14:53.548 --> 00:14:54.381
(audience chuckles)

00:14:54.381 --> 00:14:56.390
I was really delighted to hear the results

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:58.280
of the survey this morning,

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.180
because I can say some
things now that I wasn't sure

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:04.343
I could say.
(everyone laughs)

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.363
I remember Nixon.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:13.580
I actually was in
graduate school when Nixon

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:16.580
was forced to resign, I remember that.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.403
It wasn't college it was afterwards.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:25.570
I remember, I actually
remember the US being

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:31.350
both male dominated and white
dominated, quite strongly.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:36.840
But my goal was really just
to survive and not flunk.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.889
- [Beverly] Well
congratulations for doing that.

00:15:40.889 --> 00:15:41.950
(everyone laughs)

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:44.350
Even if it's 50 years later.

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:45.960
- I still remember that!

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.040
- Congressman Jackson Lee.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:52.100
Reflections on politics then and now.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:54.760
So I teach in a program that is,

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:58.540
attracts many, very
ambitious Yale students

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:01.690
who are willing to say pretty early on,

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.690
I want to grow up to
be secretary of state.

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.500
And they know that now, and
that's what they're doing.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.500
And it's great though a little terrifying.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.310
I was not that kind of Yale student.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:13.000
Were you that kind of Yale student?

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.680
You said I'm going to congress,

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.483
and I'm thinking about
politics in this moment.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:20.760
- Well thank you very much
and let me first of all say

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:24.500
how humbled I am to be
here with my sisters.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.890
We use that terminology in
the United States Congress,

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:30.300
for our fellow sisters in
the United States Congress.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.570
And, if I might, simply as I look,

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.050
see the utter power that is in this room.

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:39.880
So Yale, I do not want you
to be particularly protective

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:43.220
of this list, because I'd like
to engage with my sisters,

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.750
who are willing to do so.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:48.070
I think that this is amazing,
so I just had to get that out.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:51.170
And if you're ready to do like
Marian Wright Edelman said.

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:54.170
(audience applauds)

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:58.557
A Yale graduate who said,
"You can change the world

00:16:58.557 --> 00:16:59.690
"if you really care."

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.473
And I've just met so many of you,

00:17:01.473 --> 00:17:03.610
I think we can still change the world.

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:06.690
I was extremely naive.

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:09.030
So I think I culturally represented

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:15.010
the public school crowd,
but also some of us

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:19.340
a sheltered crowd, even coming
from the big city of New York

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:22.290
which is where I was born and raised.

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:24.840
My first year was at Washington Square.

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:26.230
I guess I'm not gonna give my biography,

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.447
but let me just quickly say at NYU.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.020
We were in the midst of a shutdown

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:35.220
that first year, as freshman.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.940
And all I could think
of is my very loving,

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:43.710
but stern parents seeing
me dragged off in handcuffs

00:17:44.917 --> 00:17:46.633
in New York in the protests.

00:17:48.928 --> 00:17:52.200
I kind of protested then
snuck back in the building

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:55.343
to tell the professor I
would get their work in.

00:17:55.343 --> 00:17:58.426
(audience chuckling)

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:06.970
I was someone who was
formulated by the words

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.090
of Dr. Martin Luther King
and never got a chance

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:11.360
to meet him, but I got
to the Southern Christian

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.100
Leadership Conference as soon as I could.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:18.350
I was asked to speak
at Juanita Abernathy's

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:19.920
funeral on Monday.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:24.470
Unfortunately, another of
our colleagues wife died

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:26.020
it's the exact same
day, but what I'm saying

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:27.983
I was very idealistic.

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:33.430
The small newspaper article in the,

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:35.610
anybody remember the New York Daily News,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.640
back then was the working man's paper,

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:39.430
working man, working woman's paper.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:41.460
That said they're opened up for Yale.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:46.087
And I really thought that
I was going for a mission.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.810
I was going to pioneer.

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.310
Really didn't know what I was formulating,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:54.620
but I knew that I was gonna get in

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:56.720
the Civil Rights Movement,
and I was going someplace

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.310
that would help formulate me.

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:01.190
So, the backdrop of the movement,

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:02.270
of the Black Power Movement,

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.540
all of that was raging at the time.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:08.530
Now, on the idealistic and naive point,

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.820
and the humorous point,
and I told Gary this,

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.293
he tries to argue me down
that they weren't here.

00:19:16.642 --> 00:19:20.010
When I drove up to the campus,

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:21.960
and many of us will remember that day,

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:26.960
the Class of '72, '71 and '72 and '73.

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:31.890
With all of the excitement
and, not teary-eyed,

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.100
well teary-eyed family members,
and the whole clan came.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:38.210
It's like three cars. (chuckles)

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:41.840
But in any event with every
clothing in my possession,

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:45.490
and trunks and suitcases.

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:49.360
And I promise you, I promise
you there was a group of men.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.590
And I laughed, I mean I guess

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:55.440
the survey that was taken,
and I'll end on this note,

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.520
we are really take charge folk.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:01.930
So even at 19, which I was,
I really didn't get offended.

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:03.210
I really thought it was humorous.

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:04.880
But they had this big, gigantic sign,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.460
Class of '70, last male class.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.730
And they were waving, and I was waving.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:13.050
I guess I didn't get the
magnitude of their conviction.

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:15.250
To make a long--
(audience laughs)

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:18.080
To make a long story short, I viewed Yale

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.163
with the challenges of being a woman,

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.500
first of woman of color.

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:26.533
A budding,

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:32.320
social activist, as

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:37.003
finessing and sharpening my skills.

00:20:37.967 --> 00:20:39.410
It was the first time I was off on my own.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:42.110
And I think many of my fellow students

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.793
were, particularly women.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:49.063
The first time that we got
confronted with sexism.

00:20:50.439 --> 00:20:51.850
And maybe we developed as a feminist,

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:56.700
or we developed as a powerful
civil rights activist,

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.180
or Black Power, whatever we crafted

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:01.810
it to be political scientist, lawyer.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:06.810
But it any event, it's the
place where I face the reality,

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:08.840
that every thing will not be

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:11.610
in a beautifully wrapped package.

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.090
And that you really are
gonna have to fight for it.

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:17.210
And I, forgive me we say
this a lot my sisters,

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:20.180
it is truly that part of Yale

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:26.437
that I think has given many of us the grit

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:29.400
to be where we are today.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.130
No matter what we might have opted to do.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:36.020
And I diminish nothing
that Yale women became,

00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:39.150
because I know what Yale
women, girls, young women,

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:40.703
went through to be here.

00:21:43.221 --> 00:21:46.221
(audience applauds)

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:53.283
- So, Dr. Vickers, in our correspondence,

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:57.100
as we were thinking about
issues that might come up

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.383
on the panel, or be worth talking about,

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:03.850
you emphasized the idea
that actually this wasn't

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:08.080
a moment neccessarily when
women had out-sized expectations

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:10.270
for what they could achieve.

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:14.970
And wasn't actually a moment
were girls, young women

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:17.340
were coming into an institution like this,

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:20.930
expecting that they could
do many of the things

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:22.770
that later generations
thought that they could do.

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:25.230
So I wonder if you could reflect upon

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:28.070
your experience on that, and particularly,

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:31.540
what the graduate school
told you about the role

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.910
that you were gonna be
expected to play in the world?

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.633
- Yes, that's quite a story.

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.770
Let me say, I went to undergraduate
school, can you hear me?

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:41.320
Are we, okay.

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:44.700
Went to undergraduate school
in 1963, Mount Holyoke College,

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:46.440
graduated in 1967.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:50.590
So I'd like to say that I
went to college in the 1950s,

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:53.281
but I graduated in the 1970s.

00:22:53.281 --> 00:22:54.710
(everyone laughs)

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.990
So consequently, it was a
time of very rapid change.

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:02.990
But I came into it as the
daughter of a working class

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:06.270
immigrant family that had worked its way

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.103
to the middle class.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:12.760
So consequently it came
with a lot of expectations

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:14.053
of young women.

00:23:15.464 --> 00:23:17.870
You know what I was sharing with Beverly,

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:21.380
there was a degree to
which I had internalized

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:25.720
these requirements so deeply
that I was, in essence,

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:29.530
not even really understanding
that I myself was,

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:32.510
though outside the box
enough to go to college

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:33.890
and to go to graduate school.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:38.890
That I was bringing with me
baggage and just embedded in me

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:42.340
kinds of baggage that it
would take many, many years.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:44.830
And indeed I doubt that I'm
still all the way out of them.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:46.500
Although I have to say Dartmouth turned me

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.944
into a real feminist.

00:23:47.944 --> 00:23:50.020
(everyone laughs)

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:54.840
But, most of all my sense was,

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.080
it was expected that I would get married.

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:59.180
The young women who were going in 1963

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:01.340
thought there were two
purposes to going to college.

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:03.350
One of which was to get an education,

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:05.790
and the other of which
was to get a husband.

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:08.530
And the purpose of the
education was to be,

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:13.530
in large part, a more educated,
successful, talented mother.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:20.890
If, for example, if you made your way into

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:24.870
that particular system, you found yourself

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:28.103
making curious selections
around things like majors.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:33.700
My SAT scores would have
suggested science and mathematics.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:38.158
I majored in French, perfume
and fashion. (laughs)

00:24:38.158 --> 00:24:38.991
(audience laughs)

00:24:38.991 --> 00:24:42.533
I mean that's, I'm being
silly, but not entirely.

00:24:44.576 --> 00:24:47.380
So consequently, that was the direction

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:50.000
I started moving in, I
had the wits to think

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.610
that I really could do
something with this,

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:55.830
because I was directing
myself at becoming a teacher.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.780
Now I wanted to be a college teacher,

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:00.430
which forced me to do advanced degrees.

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:03.600
But, I was falling in to
the sort of laundry list

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.700
of what were the expected
professions for women,

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:11.170
as a teacher, as a nurse, as a volunteer,

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:14.620
as someone with secretarial expertise.

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:17.330
There is a moment that I
can't resist recounting,

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.820
and I may even say the name of the school

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:20.780
that's involved in it.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:23.060
When I was applying to college,

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:24.940
I was applying to two
of the women's schools

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:26.610
on the East Coast.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:28.183
I came from Detroit.

00:25:29.791 --> 00:25:31.720
In the midst of applying
to those two schools

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:36.720
on the East Coast, one of
them, Smith, (chuckles)

00:25:37.407 --> 00:25:41.860
sent two of its alumni to
my middle class family home

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:43.053
in Royal Oak, Michigan.

00:25:44.989 --> 00:25:47.050
And so they came in, they
were checking me out,

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:48.330
they were checking the parents out,

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:51.093
they were checking the carpeting out.

00:25:51.093 --> 00:25:53.630
(audience laughs)

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.730
So my father, in a moment
that I will never forget.

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.140
And for me is the most
extraordinary moment

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:02.520
about knowing who my father really was,

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:05.120
looked up having been very
quiet through all of us.

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:09.457
And said, "So tell me,
what do Smith graduates do

00:26:09.457 --> 00:26:11.880
"when they graduate from Smith?"

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:16.167
And the answer was, they go to Katy Gibbs.

00:26:16.167 --> 00:26:17.830
(audience laughs and moans)

00:26:17.830 --> 00:26:19.973
This from a women's college.

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:23.920
So, yes I know it's a shocker.

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.183
And in all fairness I have to say this.

00:26:26.183 --> 00:26:29.823
It's the school that
brought you Gloria Steinem.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:33.663
It's not that it wasn't a complex place.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:38.080
And so I think that what
Beverly was mentioning,

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:41.120
is just that there was so much to overcome

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:42.490
in terms of growing out of this.

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:45.610
I think the critical
moment, going back to what

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:47.380
you were saying just a bit earlier,

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:52.140
was that turn at the moment of
the co-education of the Ivies

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:55.060
which is a sort of very complex set

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:57.350
in negative ways and positive ways.

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:00.610
And then the real sort of explosion

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:04.180
of the women's movement
in the early 1970s.

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.280
Which surrounded as it was by some of

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.320
the extraordinary moments
of the co-education

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.033
of schools like Yale and like Dartmouth,

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:16.993
where I spent 14 years taking
it through co-education.

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:20.560
It was truly shocking.

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:22.810
Now questions about graduate school.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:24.630
One of the things that was very surprising

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:26.830
about Yale Graduate
School when I was in it,

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:33.550
is simply that some of the
most talented professors,

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:35.700
the most encouraging professors,

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.500
the people who thought
the most of my work,

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:43.500
would also say things that
were just truly surprising.

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.440
For example, there was one
who at every cocktail party

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:48.477
would come up and say,
"Well you're doing so well,

00:27:48.477 --> 00:27:49.597
"isn't that grand?

00:27:49.597 --> 00:27:54.550
"But of course, the women get married,

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:57.797
'and their careers basically
end when they get married,

00:27:57.797 --> 00:28:00.037
"so it's just sad."

00:28:01.327 --> 00:28:03.810
And this was a department were 60%

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:05.403
of the students were women.

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:08.970
And I think I see Sam
Chauncey in the background.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.560
Is that Sam Chauncey? Hi Sam.

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:11.393
- It's higher.

00:28:11.393 --> 00:28:12.793
- One of my heroes.

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:16.240
And a panel way back then when I was on

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:18.420
the University Committee on Co-education,

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:21.920
I described a fellowship
in the French department

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:24.600
where 60% of the students were women.

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:27.500
And, that fellowship
was an exchange program

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:30.220
with the (speaks foreign
language) in Paris.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:32.240
So they sent an (speaks
foreign word) to Yale,

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:35.190
and Yale sent a (speaks
foreign word) to Paris.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:37.370
Every year that was a man.

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:40.810
It was restricted to the male students.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.260
Parenthetically, (speaks foreign language)

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.733
is not restricted to men students.

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:49.250
So having announced
this at some broad panel

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:50.350
where we were supposed to be making

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:53.870
I think a good impression,
I told this story,

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:57.303
and Sam fixed it instantly.

00:28:57.303 --> 00:29:00.303
(everyone applauds)

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.990
So, I'll just wrap up there.

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:10.000
But I do think we have to,
I am constantly reading

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:13.160
in myself, sort of drag on my own

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:16.963
forward moving aspirational energy.

00:29:18.062 --> 00:29:20.240
And I think as the generations go by

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:22.013
I hope we're shedding all of that.

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:26.240
- Linda, you had some,
(audience applauds)

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:28.870
I guess similar reflections
in your writing about

00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:32.340
your undergraduate years at Harvard.

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:34.430
So, I wonder if you might build off that,

00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:36.910
but then also speak a little bit about

00:29:36.910 --> 00:29:39.690
how those kinds of things began to change,

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:42.000
and the role that the law.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:45.060
These were of course years
of some rather significant

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:48.040
Supreme Court decisions in the history

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:50.610
of women's reproductive freedom,

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:52.040
as well as in other areas.

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:54.860
So I wonder if you might reflect on those.

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:56.960
- So my experience is
a little bit different.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:01.830
Although I have a Yale degree,
it was as degree from here

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:05.370
on a mid-career fellowship, I
was 10 years out of college.

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:08.830
So I went to the other
place, I went to Radcliffe,

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:10.950
because I was too old to apply to Yale.

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:12.950
I'm a Townie, I went
to Hampton High School

00:30:12.950 --> 00:30:16.210
just up the road, and
the guys went to Yale.

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:19.163
And the women made their
way to other places.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:24.340
Women at Harvard in those days were 20%

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:27.640
of the undergraduate body, it was capped.

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:29.290
We were admitted by Radcliffe,

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:32.050
we were housed by Radcliffe,
we were fed by Radcliffe.

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:34.280
Harvard really didn't
want much to do with us.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:38.010
They had to teach us,
the classes were co-ed,

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:41.160
but the social life was very segmented,

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:43.620
because the tuition and room and board,

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:44.870
well the tuition went to Harvard.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:47.760
But the room and board went to Radcliffe.

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:52.130
And we were really second
class citizens in many ways,

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:55.170
many opportunities were
closed to us in terms of

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:59.253
even access to various things on campus,

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:03.210
post-graduate fellowships and so on.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:07.860
As I told Beverly in our
earlier communication,

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:10.780
what's remarkable looking back on that

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:14.780
was how little any of us had in the way

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:18.510
of any kind of developed
feminist consciousness.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:23.120
Because the message was,
we were lucky to be there.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:26.230
And we knew that great
and cataclysmic events

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:29.600
were going on in the
country and the world.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:33.300
We were very, very conscious of Vietnam,

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:35.370
because the guys were
subject to the draft,

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:38.870
this was pre-lottery, just
everybody was gonna be drafted,

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:42.010
unless they could find some other way.

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:44.090
And we were certainly very conscious of

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.960
the Civil Rights Movement.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:47.950
Neither of those things
had anything to do,

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:49.730
as far as we could tell, with women.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:53.740
Our story wasn't visible at all.

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:56.220
And to the extent that we thought

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.223
we were being treated unfairly.

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.120
And I did think so, and I
wrote for the Harvard Crimson.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.560
And I wrote many editorials
about let us into

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.360
Lamont, the undergraduate library.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:09.093
Let us do this, let us to that.

00:32:09.990 --> 00:32:13.480
We were being treated unfairly
as Radcliffe students.

00:32:13.480 --> 00:32:16.610
We should have the same
access that you guys do.

00:32:16.610 --> 00:32:19.870
Not that we were being
treated unfairly as women.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:21.750
It sounds weird to say that.

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:24.430
I mean maybe you all understand this.

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:26.093
I have a 35 year old daughter,

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:32.790
this doesn't resonate with her.

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:34.463
I think that's one thing to say.

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:39.960
I didn't really kind of have a conscious,

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:42.570
that's how I went out
into the world of work,

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:43.620
and it was kind of interesting.

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:45.090
I didn't want to go to graduate school.

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:47.250
The assumption that Radcliffe

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.950
was that you would go to
some kind of graduate school.

00:32:51.540 --> 00:32:55.950
Not law school, maybe medical
school more than law school.

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:57.670
But arts and sciences or something.

00:32:57.670 --> 00:33:00.927
And my deans were quite
amazed when I said,

00:33:00.927 --> 00:33:03.230
"Actually I'm gonna go get a job."

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:05.130
And I got a job at the New York Times.

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:11.150
Very soon after that, the
older women at the Times,

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:13.410
and there weren't many of them,
but the ones who were there

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:15.630
were very fabulous,

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:18.670
had banded together to
file one of the early

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:22.440
Title VII sex discrimination lawsuits.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:26.490
And maybe, if you saw
the Amazon Prime show

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:29.723
about the women of
Newsweek, Good Girls Revolt,

00:33:32.145 --> 00:33:34.397
the women at Newsweek had
sort of cut the ice there

00:33:34.397 --> 00:33:36.123
and the Times was following.

00:33:37.080 --> 00:33:40.520
And we were represented
by a wonderful woman

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:43.366
named Harriet Raabe, who was,

00:33:43.366 --> 00:33:45.760
(audience applauds)
you know Harriet?

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:49.040
Who was a young law professor at Columbia,

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:52.520
and she had a sex
discrimination clinic, workshop.

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:56.400
And she was the first
female lawyer I'd ever met.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:58.630
And I thought, wow this is really,

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:00.673
there's another way to be in the world.

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:06.910
And just being part of the
class that was bringing

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:11.950
this lawsuit was a very
consciousness raising kind of thing.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:17.780
It did kind of clue me into
paying attention to law,

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:21.440
and to what was developing
out in the world.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:26.440
And I was one of a handful of
young women in the newsroom,

00:34:27.290 --> 00:34:29.890
and I got some very
interesting assignments.

00:34:29.890 --> 00:34:34.890
And I got assigned, maybe
the second year I was there,

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:37.770
to cover a press conference announcing

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:42.320
a lawsuit against New York State,

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:43.893
for the right to abortion.

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:49.920
That was one of many such
lawsuits that was going on.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:51.310
One of them would have actually come

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:52.590
to become called Roe against Wade.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:55.700
And this lawsuit never
actually got to court,

00:34:55.700 --> 00:34:58.190
because the New York
legislature changed the law,

00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:00.170
and the lawsuit became moot.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:03.233
But it got me into the
discourse about abortion.

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.330
And they asked me to write a piece

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:09.030
for the Times Sunday
Magazine, I knew no law,

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:11.930
no law whatsoever, but I
found these great women

00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:14.710
who were in civil rights
law around the city,

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:18.720
who took the trouble to educate me.

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:21.010
And I wrote a piece
for the Times Magazine,

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:22.163
and the headline was,

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:26.273
it's been widely anthologized
is how I know it,

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:29.903
Constitutional Question, Is
There a Right to Abortion?

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:35.570
Of course I argued the answer
was yes, and this was 1971.

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:37.890
Roe against Wade was two years away.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:42.810
But it kind of brought me into
a consciousness of activism

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:45.340
that I have to say in
my undergraduate years

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:48.093
was totally missing.

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:53.490
- So undergraduate years
and women's studies.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:57.570
How are today's students
taught in the context

00:35:57.570 --> 00:35:59.230
of women's studies and women's history

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:03.250
about this really critical
period from the late '60s

00:36:03.250 --> 00:36:04.920
into the early '70s?

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:08.400
What would you say is now kind
of the standard narrative?

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:10.913
And how do they think
about it and respond to it?

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:16.180
- Yeah, I want to start actually--

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:17.473
- She brought a prop. (laughs)

00:36:17.473 --> 00:36:20.680
- I have, I have something
I want to read to you,

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:23.530
from January 19, 1968.

00:36:23.530 --> 00:36:27.750
I'm not a member of any of your class,

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:30.548
but that's because I didn't try.

00:36:30.548 --> 00:36:32.390
(audience chuckling)

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:36.280
This is from Miss Laura
Caplan, Sarah Lawrence College,

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:39.763
Bronxville, New York and it's to her.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:42.790
Dear Miss Caplan.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:44.920
We have received your recent inquiry

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:48.450
and wish to let you know,
that at the present time,

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:51.450
the only women admitted to
the undergraduate programs

00:36:51.450 --> 00:36:55.570
in Yale College, are the
wives of Yale faculty members,

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:58.610
and the wives of Yale graduate students.

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:01.760
Should you be interested later
in applying for admission

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:05.210
to one of the graduate or
professional schools in Yale,

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:07.140
you should write to the dean of the school

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.430
in which you are interested
for further information.

00:37:10.430 --> 00:37:15.430
Sincerely yours, John Muskins,
Jr., director of admissions.

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:19.680
This was weeks before

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:23.081
admissions was open to women at Yale.

00:37:23.081 --> 00:37:23.914
(audience moans)

00:37:23.914 --> 00:37:27.650
And my special advisor
at Sarah Lawrence College

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:31.173
was Jackie Madfeld, who later
became president of Barnard.

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:37.670
Knew Elga Wasserman, and
knew this was coming.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:39.500
And actually I'd had a lot off discussions

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:41.100
with Jackie about it.

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:46.100
So, I knew that Yale was
about to make this decision.

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:50.000
And yet, the answer I got when I inquired

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.050
about how to apply was
not, well just hold on

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:56.975
for a few weeks, it was, "Well,
you could marry a Yale man."

00:37:56.975 --> 00:37:58.760
(everyone laughs)

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:02.370
Or, you could apply later
when you're thinking

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:03.570
about graduate school.

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:06.780
And that seemed, it's not a joke.

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:10.500
And I do now have a Yale
degree, because when I became,

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:12.590
if you become a tenured professor at Yale,

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.410
you got a Yale degree.

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:17.180
You can't even be a
tenured professor at Yale

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:18.930
without a Yale degree. (laughs)

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.187
Right, yeah, that's amazing.
- Only the best and

00:38:20.187 --> 00:38:21.670
the brightest, yes.

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:24.930
- But I put it on my door when I became

00:38:24.930 --> 00:38:26.840
chair of women's studies.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:30.630
And co-chair of the Yale faculty forum.

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:33.400
And I intend to donate it (laughs)

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:35.390
to the history archive here.

00:38:35.390 --> 00:38:39.163
But it frames how we were seen.

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:45.620
That it seemed okay to answer
an inquiry for admission

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:50.350
that way is kind of really important.

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:53.940
Because, it shows that for whatever one of

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:57.790
these places here, elsewhere that we went,

00:38:57.790 --> 00:39:01.840
we gained admission,
but not really access,

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:03.680
not really access.

00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:06.900
And, it took years and years to learn

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.360
what was the difference.

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:12.360
So, I want to answer the question of how

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:14.653
I became a feminist in and around Yale,

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:17.330
and then talk about the current students.

00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:20.040
I taught a wonderful seminar last year

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:22.510
on co-educating Yale.

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:26.800
We had amazing discussions
with the undergraduates now.

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:29.410
And I want to say some of
the things that they think

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:32.430
and some of the ways that
women's gender and sexuality.

00:39:32.430 --> 00:39:37.057
'Cause it's no longer
women's studies handles this.

00:39:38.550 --> 00:39:42.493
But I became a feminist
in and around Yale also.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:47.360
But it was in the Fall of 1969

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:53.037
when I came to a march to plan

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:59.610
the rally in May, the
Panthers rally in May.

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:03.443
And this happened in
late November of 1969.

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:06.370
There were

00:40:07.580 --> 00:40:11.340
a number of Panther
women, who were pregnant

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:13.493
and who were in jail.

00:40:15.131 --> 00:40:18.730
And the famous, and Linda I'm forgetting

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:21.070
the name of the Connecticut
case on abortion.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:22.080
What's the name of the thing?

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:23.970
- Abele against Markle.

00:40:23.970 --> 00:40:25.830
- Had just been decided.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:30.390
And there were a lot of
feminists who were working on

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.680
questions of pregnancy
discrimination, abortion,

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:38.793
access to contraception information.

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:43.410
It feels like hundreds of
years ago, but it wasn't.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:46.690
And this group of people, the Panthers,

00:40:46.690 --> 00:40:51.160
the people around what would
be the trial in the spring,

00:40:51.160 --> 00:40:53.550
and the people involved in

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:58.770
access to health, came
together in the fall.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:02.070
The Panther women created what they called

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:03.840
a women's brigade.

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:08.450
And it was the first time
in many years of marching

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:11.670
for anti-war and civil
rights that I ever marched,

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:14.440
that they took the women
out of the general march

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:16.490
and put us in front.

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:19.440
And we marched as a women's
brigade around New Haven,

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.330
through the hill, through the green,

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:23.373
and up to the steps of the courthouse.

00:41:24.559 --> 00:41:27.600
And we were chanting, obscenity Yale,

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.650
obscenity Harvard obscenity Yale,

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:31.834
get the Panthers out of jail.

00:41:31.834 --> 00:41:33.283
(everyone laughs)
All around there.

00:41:34.580 --> 00:41:38.293
It was the exhilaration of that,

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:42.700
and the really kind of deep training

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:45.293
by the women activists
and the Panther women,

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:49.900
in that moment that gave me the name

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:51.570
for what I already was.

00:41:51.570 --> 00:41:54.720
And it was the name
feminism that allowed me

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:56.570
to recognize myself as a feminist.

00:41:56.570 --> 00:41:58.250
It's not that I changed,

00:41:58.250 --> 00:42:01.610
it's that it got a category in the world.

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:04.760
I subsequently lived my life in and around

00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:06.190
Yale and New Haven.

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:10.610
And I used to drive around
the green for years,

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:13.350
for like 18 years, trying to figure out

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:16.670
about a particular moment that happened.

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:18.960
The women were on the
steps of the courthouse

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:20.283
at the end of this march.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:25.240
We were gleeful, we were exhilarated,

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:27.370
we had found companions.

00:42:27.370 --> 00:42:29.430
There was gonna be something to be done

00:42:29.430 --> 00:42:31.040
about this situation.

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:34.280
And people were taking our
pictures in front of us.

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:38.090
And somebody pointed up
to the left on a rooftop

00:42:38.090 --> 00:42:42.627
and said, "Look, are
those guns are cameras?"

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:47.270
Whether or not there were
sharpshooters on the roof,

00:42:47.270 --> 00:42:50.180
whether or not they were guns or cameras

00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:53.080
aimed at us, we never knew.

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:55.800
And I spent years driving around the green

00:42:55.800 --> 00:42:58.560
'til I finally figured out why I couldn't

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:02.130
reconfigure the geography of that moment.

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:06.130
And it was because the
Connecticut Financial Center.

00:43:06.130 --> 00:43:08.401
The building is gone and the Connecticut

00:43:08.401 --> 00:43:09.890
(everyone laughs)
Financial Center is there.

00:43:09.890 --> 00:43:12.550
So you can't recreate the geometry.

00:43:12.550 --> 00:43:17.550
But that moment was my
deepest political education.

00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:22.410
And it happened in town, not in Yale.

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:25.820
And it was directed by the women Panthers

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:28.980
and the activists from
all over New England

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:30.900
who came to try to figure
out what to do about

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:35.900
the women Panthers who
were pregnant and in jail.

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:38.910
What I would argue, and what I try to do

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:40.950
within women's studies here at Yale,

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:42.870
Women, Gender and Sexuality Studies,

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:45.853
which is very important to recognize that

00:43:45.853 --> 00:43:48.060
that has changed, is

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:54.600
give some permission for
that wider world to happen

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:58.973
within the university,
as well as outside of it.

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:02.870
Adrian Rich wrote an
essay around that time,

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:05.270
Towards a Women Centered University.

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:09.700
And it articulated what kind
of university would have

00:44:09.700 --> 00:44:13.400
to come into being, in
order for there truly

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:15.430
to be co-education.

00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:18.480
So the women students
now, and the men students,

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:22.830
and the people that are
gender fluid and don't

00:44:22.830 --> 00:44:27.260
go by those designations,
do not actually think that

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:29.740
co-education has happened.

00:44:29.740 --> 00:44:32.350
They think it's a work in progress.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:34.993
And, they think that,

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:41.380
they still are not seeing

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:45.120
the way that they would like to be seen,

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:48.620
as the education part of the co, okay.

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:50.440
Think about the word co-education.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:54.650
Co means together, and for
much of that first year

00:44:54.650 --> 00:44:56.910
there was a lot of
debate about how the men

00:44:56.910 --> 00:44:58.940
and women students should,
or shouldn't, or could,

00:44:58.940 --> 00:45:01.550
or could be, or not together.

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:05.070
But it's the question of
education that was really key.

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:06.870
The question of what
difference did it make?

00:45:06.870 --> 00:45:10.170
Not to Yale, not to the
dorms, not to the bathrooms,

00:45:10.170 --> 00:45:13.660
but to the country that
we were all here together.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:16.220
And I would argue that momentarily,

00:45:16.220 --> 00:45:21.220
for a short period of time,
co-education descended on Yale

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:24.690
on the green, for a little while.

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:28.830
And then it left, that
it appears occasionally

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:33.170
when we are seen as citizens
needing an education.

00:45:33.170 --> 00:45:37.060
Rather than as men or
women, or whatever else

00:45:37.060 --> 00:45:38.783
you want to be,

00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:43.920
trying to struggle about
how we live together.

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:48.650
So, that's what under my hand,

00:45:48.650 --> 00:45:53.090
Women, Gender and Sexuality
Studies tries to be about.

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:56.360
People are yearning for this education,

00:45:56.360 --> 00:46:01.360
people are yearning to be
seen as citizens together.

00:46:01.570 --> 00:46:03.653
And that's what I think it means.

00:46:06.161 --> 00:46:09.161
(audience applauds)

00:46:13.134 --> 00:46:15.980
- Well I want to pose one more
question to the whole panel

00:46:15.980 --> 00:46:19.000
before we open it up to the audience.

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:21.720
And that really is the
comparative question

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:26.720
that I raised in my introductory remarks.

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:29.830
What can we learn about the present

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:34.300
by looking back to this
moment of the late 1960s

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:39.190
and early 1970s, and really
moving forth in the 1970s?

00:46:39.190 --> 00:46:44.190
The 1970s seems like a very
paradoxical decade in retrospect

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:47.500
in which you both got really the rise

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:49.010
of feminist consciousness,

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:52.400
the rise of a very
powerful women's movement,

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:57.400
changes in ideas about
sexuality, about sexual identity,

00:46:58.390 --> 00:47:00.920
liberation movements flowering.

00:47:00.920 --> 00:47:03.480
And then it was also a
decade in which you saw

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:07.100
a very powerful conservative
movement taking hold,

00:47:07.100 --> 00:47:11.750
really culminating in the
election of Ronald Reagan in 1980.

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:15.470
These things were
happening simultaneously.

00:47:15.470 --> 00:47:17.070
They were often clashing with each other,

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:19.720
they were constituting each other.

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:23.410
And it seems that we
may be back in a moment,

00:47:23.410 --> 00:47:25.180
perhaps a little bit like that.

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:30.110
Where you have powerful
energies on all sides.

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:32.970
It's not clear, as it wasn't in the '70s

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:36.230
which of those energies might win out.

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:39.210
And I think for awhile we might have told

00:47:39.210 --> 00:47:42.360
the story of the '60s into the '70s,

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:45.610
as a kind of progress story,
as a simple progress story

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:48.590
from a kind of liberal perspective.

00:47:48.590 --> 00:47:51.670
Is that still how you
think about those years?

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:55.190
But really this question
of comparing then and now.

00:47:55.190 --> 00:47:58.920
And particularly what
we might take away from

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:00.570
this earlier moment that might tell us

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:01.970
something about today.

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:05.277
And really anyone who
wants to jump in on this.

00:48:05.277 --> 00:48:07.400
- Can I jump in on this?

00:48:07.400 --> 00:48:10.010
This may not be what you want to hear.

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:12.173
And there might be disagreement among us.

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:17.730
I think a lot of us were very,

00:48:17.730 --> 00:48:20.603
I don't know if the right
word is naive, innocent.

00:48:21.710 --> 00:48:23.630
Some of us were 18 when we came in,

00:48:23.630 --> 00:48:27.900
you were 19, I'm 17.

00:48:27.900 --> 00:48:29.817
We really thought we
really changed the world.

00:48:29.817 --> 00:48:31.740
And I think a lot of it has to do

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:34.743
with all of these other things
also happening in the '60s.

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:39.433
As I tell my students now,
and I'm still teaching,

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:45.780
we probably exaggerated
how much power we had then.

00:48:45.780 --> 00:48:50.000
They vastly underestimate
how much power they have now.

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:51.840
This is both the men and the women.

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:54.370
I remember a year ago in
a class I was teaching,

00:48:54.370 --> 00:48:56.020
there were about 50 students in the class.

00:48:56.020 --> 00:48:58.340
I said, I was getting
more an more agitated.

00:48:58.340 --> 00:49:03.210
And I said, "Okay," I was sort
of talking about politics,

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:05.237
power, et cetera and I said,

00:49:05.237 --> 00:49:08.520
"Well how many of you
feel you have any power?"

00:49:08.520 --> 00:49:10.940
No hands went up.

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:14.420
I repeated it, "I said no,
no, no, how about slightly?"

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:17.950
Two guys, two men, mostly
women in the class,

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:20.563
two men raised their
hands up 'til about here.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.647
Two out of this entire
class, and I said, "Okay,

00:49:25.647 --> 00:49:27.210
"let's start with you two."

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:28.873
And one of them said,

00:49:30.247 --> 00:49:33.317
"I think I have some power over myself."

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:37.057
I" don't have any power
to effect anybody else,

00:49:37.057 --> 00:49:37.900
"or anything else."

00:49:37.900 --> 00:49:39.970
And the other one said, "Just like him."

00:49:39.970 --> 00:49:41.480
And that was it.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:45.360
So, I don't know, I have
taught at many universities,

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:46.833
both in the US and abroad.

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:51.060
Yale may be different,
but I don't think so,

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:52.300
not in this way.

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:54.380
The other thing I have
noticed over the last,

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:56.990
I don't know, 10, 20
years, is more and more

00:49:56.990 --> 00:50:00.240
of the women students
resist the label feminist.

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:03.480
So, they may say I'm a post feminist,

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.980
they may rephrase the question.

00:50:05.980 --> 00:50:08.850
Do I believe that women
should have all the rights

00:50:08.850 --> 00:50:10.330
and access, et cetera?

00:50:10.330 --> 00:50:11.500
Yes.

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:13.857
And I said, "That doesn't
make you a feminist?"

00:50:13.857 --> 00:50:14.887
"Nope."

00:50:16.530 --> 00:50:18.030
I don't know what to do with this.

00:50:18.030 --> 00:50:19.980
I actually think we're going backwards.

00:50:21.040 --> 00:50:22.880
And you may not want to hear that,

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:24.530
but I think we're going backwards.

00:50:24.530 --> 00:50:26.920
I've been in the academy my entire life.

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:28.771
I think we are going backwards.

00:50:28.771 --> 00:50:31.771
(audience applauds)

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:37.580
- No, I have to say that
every time I watch the news,

00:50:37.580 --> 00:50:41.150
I say to myself, good grief,
here's what I worked for

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:43.563
my whole life and we're going to lose it.

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:47.880
You know I don't have
much more to say to that.

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:48.810
But it is

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:53.740
truly upsetting, I'm
more upset and depressed.

00:50:53.740 --> 00:50:58.170
In the '60s and '70s we had the sense

00:50:58.170 --> 00:51:00.710
that we could do something to fix it.

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:03.363
And here I'm looking at Congresswoman Lee,

00:51:04.810 --> 00:51:06.920
and what she's trying to do.

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:08.940
And you hang in there,

00:51:08.940 --> 00:51:13.940
despite any amount of pushback,
which is so admirable.

00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:17.290
But, I just, I loved to hear what you have

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:20.720
to say about this, but I just wish I could

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:23.950
have the same, maybe I was
just younger, who knows.

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:26.690
But the kind of energy
we had during those years

00:51:26.690 --> 00:51:30.433
around many, many important causes,

00:51:31.830 --> 00:51:33.943
and feminism certainly being one of them,

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:39.170
seems to me to be somehow lost.

00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:42.440
And I sense that we're moving backwards.

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:44.643
- Well you've given me
a very good opening.

00:51:47.243 --> 00:51:52.243
Let me build on your words
and Laura's words in terms of,

00:51:53.210 --> 00:51:56.650
and the moderator's words
in terms of this comparative

00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:01.330
bubble that we're in.

00:52:01.330 --> 00:52:04.480
I am, to build on what
I said coming in naive,

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:07.860
but coming in believing
that I was going to be

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:09.740
a tree shaker and a change maker,

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:12.380
in terms of having gotten
into Yale in the first place,

00:52:12.380 --> 00:52:16.083
from the very meager
background that I came from.

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:22.320
You were in '69, we had
not gotten there yet,

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:26.470
but we were in '70 Laura,
you were the Spring of '60,

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:28.509
you were saying '69 or '70?

00:52:28.509 --> 00:52:31.270
- I was here for the march,
but I graduated in '70.

00:52:31.270 --> 00:52:32.970
- Right, but you were here from--

00:52:32.970 --> 00:52:34.130
- Yes.
- Right.

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:38.860
So, I'm gonna speak from
'70, which was the time

00:52:38.860 --> 00:52:41.640
when the Weathermen came,
and it was in the backdrop

00:52:41.640 --> 00:52:44.263
of the Bobby Seale trial, as I recollect.

00:52:46.573 --> 00:52:49.520
Caymen Brewster made the decision to keep

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:51.680
the university open.

00:52:51.680 --> 00:52:56.280
For those of you in
classes, '71, '72 and '73

00:52:56.280 --> 00:53:00.780
was in the backdrop of
students being killed

00:53:00.780 --> 00:53:04.840
at Kent State, literally
shot dead on the campus.

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:08.823
It could have been a catastrophic,

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:15.243
similar to '69, catastrophic
implosion, if you will.

00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:20.630
Many of my fellow women students

00:53:20.630 --> 00:53:23.060
who were in their own movement,

00:53:23.060 --> 00:53:25.300
myself who was in the Black Power Movement

00:53:25.300 --> 00:53:28.950
when I say that with the
African American House,

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.203
with the Black Student's Association.

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:37.270
And other groups of color,
Native Americans, et cetera,

00:53:37.490 --> 00:53:39.950
this became the center point

00:53:39.950 --> 00:53:44.880
of persons converging sort of the children

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:47.980
of the great society who have benefited,

00:53:47.980 --> 00:53:51.040
but yet hated Vietnam, were
all coming to this campus.

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:54.370
I still maintain the sense that we were in

00:53:54.370 --> 00:53:59.160
the midst of change, even though
Richard Nixon was president

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:01.450
we still had that hope,

00:54:01.450 --> 00:54:06.210
that we could diversify, we
could get an African American

00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:07.760
studies program, which we did.

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:10.270
Yale should be very
proud of being a mother

00:54:10.270 --> 00:54:12.320
of African American
Studies, and gave birth

00:54:12.320 --> 00:54:15.273
to Dr. Skip Lewis Gates,
or Lewis Gibb Gates.

00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:21.350
You can tell him I said
Waverly went off to Harvard,

00:54:21.350 --> 00:54:25.200
but in any event, as an
outstanding international,

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:27.570
and national program on history.

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:31.400
But came out of this,
that was a male student.

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:35.160
What I'm saying is that
I felt, in our experience

00:54:35.160 --> 00:54:38.733
in that time, it was a changing time.

00:54:39.650 --> 00:54:43.500
It was an explosive time
and your time they were

00:54:45.200 --> 00:54:49.360
marksmen that we could
see right on the campus.

00:54:49.360 --> 00:54:51.660
There was some dispute
with one of my sisters here

00:54:51.660 --> 00:54:53.150
that said they were outside.

00:54:53.150 --> 00:54:56.170
I saw the National Guard on the inside.

00:54:56.170 --> 00:55:01.170
Meaning when I said inside
the perimeter of our campus

00:55:01.490 --> 00:55:02.850
standing side by side,

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:04.120
because they were
expecting the Weathermen.

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:07.963
I use that description to
say, it was a colorful moment.

00:55:08.860 --> 00:55:12.120
But it was a moment when we
thought that we were there

00:55:12.120 --> 00:55:15.640
really driving the political
change of the nation.

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:17.980
That we were standing
up to what we thought

00:55:17.980 --> 00:55:20.810
was an unfair trial of a Black Panther.

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:22.620
And I'm glad you talked
about pregnant women,

00:55:22.620 --> 00:55:25.563
I do a lot of work on that,
pregnant women incarcerated.

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:32.060
So, for me, I was infused with change.

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:37.233
It was growing.

00:55:38.450 --> 00:55:43.450
I was a deacon in the Yale
Church for the very reason

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:45.940
just to hear Sloan Coffin talk about

00:55:48.033 --> 00:55:51.513
the biblical connections,
so he really was societal.

00:55:52.460 --> 00:55:54.530
He helped me go and work with

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:56.180
the Southern Christian
Leadership Conference.

00:55:56.180 --> 00:56:00.450
And so during the summer breaks,

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:04.450
loaded with what happened
in the Spring of 1970,

00:56:04.450 --> 00:56:06.553
loaded with that, ready for change.

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:09.490
And realizing that there were

00:56:10.750 --> 00:56:12.720
African American blacks in the South,

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:15.090
in spite of the Voting Rights Act,

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:17.660
who were frightened and
panicked about voting.

00:56:17.660 --> 00:56:20.400
We went down to register
people on plantations,

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:22.510
which existed where sharecroppers were.

00:56:22.510 --> 00:56:24.313
I was infused with that spirit.

00:56:25.580 --> 00:56:29.820
I think that we have a challenge.

00:56:29.820 --> 00:56:33.520
Because I compare '69.

00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:34.600
Which may be

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:38.823
candy canes,

00:56:40.363 --> 00:56:43.573
bubbles in water, compared
to where we are today.

00:56:46.350 --> 00:56:49.030
This is an important moment in pause

00:56:49.030 --> 00:56:53.290
in the driving spirit that I have,

00:56:53.290 --> 00:56:55.150
which I'd like to attribute to family.

00:56:55.150 --> 00:56:58.350
But I'd like to think
it is to these moments

00:56:58.350 --> 00:57:00.103
that I had on this campus.

00:57:01.660 --> 00:57:03.720
Too bad that we had
this long period of time

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:05.900
we've all gone off,
when I look at the array

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:07.053
of powers I said.

00:57:08.320 --> 00:57:11.400
I must get this in before the questions.

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:15.693
There is a toxicity that
exists now as I said before,

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:19.100
that I don't know could
even compare to '69.

00:57:19.100 --> 00:57:22.020
In '69 whether it was
feminism or otherwise,

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:23.420
it was in front of us and we said

00:57:23.420 --> 00:57:25.193
we can do something about it.

00:57:26.300 --> 00:57:29.000
I think we have been as
wonderful parents and adults,

00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:31.470
and probably have not been as constructive

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:36.470
in infusing in that rush
for those who are younger.

00:57:37.230 --> 00:57:39.360
Let me give the faith
of Black Lives Matter,

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:41.510
let me give the faith
of March for Our Lives,

00:57:41.510 --> 00:57:44.170
let me give me the faith of
those who are now organizing

00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:47.260
as young as seven, and eight
and nine on climate change.

00:57:47.260 --> 00:57:48.440
There is something there.

00:57:48.440 --> 00:57:51.400
We shouldn't be so partial in ourselves.

00:57:51.400 --> 00:57:54.490
But I do think what we still
have the opportunity to do

00:57:55.650 --> 00:58:00.207
is to remedy the fact that we're in 2019

00:58:00.207 --> 00:58:03.380
and the Equal Rights Amendment
has not yet been passed.

00:58:03.380 --> 00:58:06.350
So what were we doing just introducing it,

00:58:06.350 --> 00:58:07.260
and introducing it.

00:58:07.260 --> 00:58:12.040
We can remedy the fact that
although the preciousness

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:15.110
and the little book
called the Constitution

00:58:15.110 --> 00:58:17.480
should be in everybody's
purse, Like Barbara Jordan,

00:58:17.480 --> 00:58:20.210
Courtney and Shirley Chisholm, we're not.

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:23.140
And so our faith gets

00:58:24.390 --> 00:58:29.390
weakened when we see
the massive reconstruct

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:32.320
of what has been the model of democracy

00:58:32.320 --> 00:58:34.330
with all of its warts.

00:58:34.330 --> 00:58:37.740
And so what I would say the comparison is

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:39.337
that as we look for it in '69,

00:58:42.500 --> 00:58:44.647
we basically were looking
for it and saying,

00:58:44.647 --> 00:58:46.110
"I know we can make a difference."

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:49.615
Whatever our spirit was, it
might not have been perfect.

00:58:49.615 --> 00:58:51.200
The treatment might not have been perfect.

00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:55.800
But I still had hope coming out '69, '70

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:58.960
that we were going to be change makers

00:58:58.960 --> 00:59:02.030
in whatever we're doing to compare it now.

00:59:02.030 --> 00:59:06.913
And I want to say to, if
we don't reignite the fire,

00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:12.430
the world is watching whether
or not we are collapsing

00:59:12.430 --> 00:59:16.970
what they have come to know
as a marker for democracy

00:59:16.970 --> 00:59:19.420
for human rights, for the unempowered.

00:59:19.420 --> 00:59:23.990
One man, you know that
Yale gets sensitive,

00:59:23.990 --> 00:59:27.740
but one man is trying to deconstruct.

00:59:27.740 --> 00:59:29.800
And he's deconstructing
the treatment of women,

00:59:29.800 --> 00:59:31.980
the respect of women,
the dignity of women,

00:59:31.980 --> 00:59:34.690
one man, one man.

00:59:34.690 --> 00:59:39.690
Another man is adding to
that by refusing to pass

00:59:40.120 --> 00:59:42.810
the bill that I wrote, the
Violence Against Women Act

00:59:42.810 --> 00:59:44.280
that has been renewed year, after year,

00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:45.730
after year when it's expired.

00:59:48.790 --> 00:59:49.900
I have tried to maintain.

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:52.040
You ask the question, how can we do it,

00:59:52.040 --> 00:59:53.990
women in the United States Congress,

00:59:53.990 --> 00:59:57.050
others in the United States
Congress who are there,

00:59:57.050 --> 01:00:01.260
who look like we're in the midst of fire.

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:05.250
It is every day we're meeting
and reminding ourselves

01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:07.930
of the major burden that we have

01:00:07.930 --> 01:00:11.140
that we don't consider a pain, a burden,

01:00:11.140 --> 01:00:13.330
to put it back together.

01:00:13.330 --> 01:00:16.350
To not let them get the best of us, them.

01:00:16.350 --> 01:00:18.020
Not let them get the best of us,

01:00:18.020 --> 01:00:20.830
let America get the best
of us, forgive me for this,

01:00:20.830 --> 01:00:25.660
but we live it every day and
I still have to push forward

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:29.350
to carry that '69 fever that

01:00:30.750 --> 01:00:33.740
colleague, they weren't
colleagues, fellow students,

01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:36.500
that standing out in the midst of 1970,

01:00:36.500 --> 01:00:39.400
when this university was
shut down and opened up

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:43.500
to every man or SDS,
Weatherman, Black Power,

01:00:43.500 --> 01:00:46.183
every other group that
came and we survived it.

01:00:47.390 --> 01:00:50.260
And I'm glad Virginia is
here, because Latino women

01:00:50.260 --> 01:00:52.070
are emerging, Native
American women are now

01:00:52.070 --> 01:00:55.310
in the United States Congress
for the first time in numbers.

01:00:55.310 --> 01:00:59.050
But we were surging because we believed

01:00:59.050 --> 01:01:00.590
that we could be change makers.

01:01:00.590 --> 01:01:03.590
If we don't leave here
in whatever category

01:01:03.590 --> 01:01:08.110
of life that we're in,
and get that little fire,

01:01:08.110 --> 01:01:10.850
so that we can spread
it across the nation,

01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:13.823
I don't want to see us again
on the other side of 2020,

01:01:14.690 --> 01:01:16.250
when we should have done our jobs.

01:01:16.250 --> 01:01:17.550
Forgive me for that editorial comment.

01:01:17.550 --> 01:01:20.030
But that's what I've seen,
that's what I've seen

01:01:21.335 --> 01:01:22.643
(audience applauds)
That's what I've seen.

01:01:23.780 --> 01:01:26.930
I'm saying let's go
together, let's go together.

01:01:26.930 --> 01:01:29.930
(audience applauds)

01:01:32.389 --> 01:01:36.660
- Do you want to add anything
before we open it up?

01:01:36.660 --> 01:01:39.260
- I want to hear what Laura
thinks of (voice fades).

01:01:41.930 --> 01:01:44.750
Do they also say they're post feminist?

01:01:44.750 --> 01:01:46.021
- Do they say what?

01:01:46.021 --> 01:01:46.860
- They're post feminists.

01:01:46.860 --> 01:01:51.090
- Well, they've been post
feminists for a long time.

01:01:51.090 --> 01:01:55.640
After 2008, it has shifted again,

01:01:55.640 --> 01:02:00.060
when very fundamental
questions could again be asked.

01:02:00.060 --> 01:02:04.897
I will say that Susan Faludi
wrote a book about "Backlash."

01:02:06.206 --> 01:02:09.610
Soon after this period of
time in which she traced.

01:02:09.610 --> 01:02:14.590
It's not only that we
didn't fully do our job,

01:02:14.590 --> 01:02:18.280
or we couldn't keep
the burden of it going.

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:22.010
People got hurt in those
times, and actually also needed

01:02:22.010 --> 01:02:25.340
a break, all kinds of things
happen at a time like that.

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:27.330
There was active resistance.

01:02:27.330 --> 01:02:31.580
There are students at Yale who
we know were being advised,

01:02:31.580 --> 01:02:35.290
well you don't want to, don't
major in Women's Studies,

01:02:35.290 --> 01:02:38.000
you don't want to have
anything to do with that.

01:02:38.000 --> 01:02:41.210
And that went on for a
really long period of time.

01:02:41.210 --> 01:02:45.010
And in 2008, there's a palpable shift,

01:02:45.010 --> 01:02:47.857
where students really wanted to know why,

01:02:50.660 --> 01:02:54.020
why the crisis happened, why their parents

01:02:54.020 --> 01:02:57.620
or the parent's of their
roommates lost their house,

01:02:57.620 --> 01:02:59.180
what there was to do about it,

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:04.180
began to ask those
fundamental questions again.

01:03:06.230 --> 01:03:10.060
I don't think the students
are post feminist here now.

01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:13.420
They were, it was very
hard to talk to them.

01:03:13.420 --> 01:03:15.333
What happens is what you described.

01:03:16.490 --> 01:03:18.907
They go out to work and
they come back and they say,

01:03:18.907 --> 01:03:21.455
"Oh my god, why didn't you tell me?"

01:03:21.455 --> 01:03:22.777
(audience laughs)

01:03:22.777 --> 01:03:24.050
- The fight is still on.
- And we say, yeah,

01:03:24.050 --> 01:03:26.297
and we say, "Well you know we're here,

01:03:26.297 --> 01:03:29.080
"we're here, we were here."

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:31.520
I think there's something
very important about that.

01:03:31.520 --> 01:03:33.620
I honor that actually.

01:03:33.620 --> 01:03:36.410
Young people come to Yale with dreams,

01:03:36.410 --> 01:03:40.130
some of them dream that things
will be easier for them,

01:03:40.130 --> 01:03:42.010
because of Yale.

01:03:42.010 --> 01:03:45.060
Some of them dream this is a magic palace

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:47.330
with all of these stone buildings,

01:03:47.330 --> 01:03:50.273
and it will change their lives.

01:03:52.124 --> 01:03:55.300
That dream has to be honored.

01:03:55.300 --> 01:04:00.300
That dream gets broken, and
feminism has to be there,

01:04:00.640 --> 01:04:03.850
both to honor the wish

01:04:03.850 --> 01:04:07.340
to transcend the contradictions
and pains of our society.

01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:10.947
And to be there to say,
"Well no now you see

01:04:10.947 --> 01:04:11.977
"that didn't work.

01:04:11.977 --> 01:04:14.467
"That's okay, let's see what other people

01:04:14.467 --> 01:04:15.790
"have thought about it."

01:04:15.790 --> 01:04:18.590
So I don't think it's
a post feminist time,

01:04:18.590 --> 01:04:20.200
at least at Yale.

01:04:20.200 --> 01:04:21.850
I think people are

01:04:23.650 --> 01:04:25.830
disgruntled, I think they feel

01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:29.640
that things are not working out right.

01:04:29.640 --> 01:04:30.903
They're right about that.

01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:36.240
I think post feminism,
at least in the classes

01:04:36.240 --> 01:04:39.443
that I get near, isn't there.

01:04:41.713 --> 01:04:42.820
- Do you want to add anything briefly?

01:04:42.820 --> 01:04:44.970
I want to make sure we get to the crowd.

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:49.500
- Just kind of briefly,
maybe one way of phrasing

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:51.603
the unfilled goal.

01:04:52.940 --> 01:04:55.670
So looking back at the
Harvard and Radcliffe,

01:04:55.670 --> 01:04:58.730
or the entry of relatively small number

01:04:58.730 --> 01:05:00.610
of women into Yale.

01:05:00.610 --> 01:05:03.810
So, the default mode of course was male.

01:05:03.810 --> 01:05:05.870
And male was the norm,

01:05:05.870 --> 01:05:09.980
and women were the
deviation from the norm.

01:05:09.980 --> 01:05:13.410
And maybe the smart
girls in high school now,

01:05:13.410 --> 01:05:16.520
and girls run things in high school now,

01:05:16.520 --> 01:05:20.270
they don't grow up thinking
that they are nothing,

01:05:20.270 --> 01:05:22.480
or that to be a women is not the norm.

01:05:22.480 --> 01:05:24.480
But they'll find that out pretty quickly

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:26.880
that the default mode is male,

01:05:26.880 --> 01:05:29.740
we're seeing that play out in
our politics now and so on.

01:05:29.740 --> 01:05:34.740
So maybe a goal that we
can draw from our own

01:05:35.580 --> 01:05:38.790
lived experience going
forward is to try to create

01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:43.790
a world where the default mode is to be

01:05:43.890 --> 01:05:46.060
an adult person in the world.

01:05:46.060 --> 01:05:48.663
And it's not the assumption that,

01:05:54.180 --> 01:05:58.670
it's the male leadership, it's
the male running the show.

01:05:58.670 --> 01:06:03.540
And I think that was certainly
what most of us felt,

01:06:03.540 --> 01:06:07.250
and what many of us
learn in the real world

01:06:08.097 --> 01:06:09.497
once we left the Ivy League.

01:06:10.920 --> 01:06:12.520
- So I want to take the chance now

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:14.250
to open up the conversation.

01:06:14.250 --> 01:06:17.380
I have two requests.

01:06:17.380 --> 01:06:19.700
One is that you introduce yourself

01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:22.620
and maybe your year from Yale.

01:06:22.620 --> 01:06:25.340
Two is that particularly
given the nature of the topic,

01:06:25.340 --> 01:06:29.083
we avoid making long political speeches.

01:06:30.966 --> 01:06:35.070
And we'll try to take a
few questions and comments

01:06:35.070 --> 01:06:38.333
in a group, and then open
it up to response up here.

01:06:39.756 --> 01:06:40.589
Are there any?

01:06:43.090 --> 01:06:45.190
One starting out right here in the middle.

01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:47.390
Think there's a microphone.

01:06:47.390 --> 01:06:48.693
- There's a microphone coming

01:06:48.693 --> 01:06:52.756
your way.
- There's a mic coming.

01:06:52.756 --> 01:06:55.046
- I loved your last comment about maybe--

01:06:55.046 --> 01:06:56.690
- [Beverly] Could you introduce yourself?

01:06:56.690 --> 01:06:59.810
- Judy Silverstein, Branford '72.

01:06:59.810 --> 01:07:03.800
I loved your last comment
about maybe it won't be about

01:07:03.800 --> 01:07:06.463
women or men in the world, but adults.

01:07:07.560 --> 01:07:11.647
Could you comment on
what the change now is,

01:07:11.647 --> 01:07:13.380
and what it means to be an adult?

01:07:13.380 --> 01:07:16.790
Because I don't think,
even though we're in a more

01:07:16.790 --> 01:07:21.180
male normed, adult world,

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:25.120
the male isn't the same male
that was back in our day,

01:07:25.120 --> 01:07:28.510
or our parent's day, or grandparent's day.

01:07:28.510 --> 01:07:31.910
And particularly those of
you who think about gender

01:07:31.910 --> 01:07:36.480
and sexuality, what does it
mean to be a non-gendered adult

01:07:36.480 --> 01:07:38.743
today versus at another time?

01:07:40.742 --> 01:07:41.575
- Are there any other

01:07:41.575 --> 01:07:43.367
queries.
- Good question, yeah um--

01:07:43.367 --> 01:07:44.570
- No, no wait a second.

01:07:44.570 --> 01:07:46.420
Let's just get a couple of comments

01:07:46.420 --> 01:07:48.673
and then we'll open it up.

01:07:51.520 --> 01:07:53.630
- I just want to thank
you for your question.

01:07:53.630 --> 01:07:56.200
Oh, I'm Patricia Joyce Smith Carter,

01:07:56.200 --> 01:07:57.650
and I've worked under all those names,

01:07:57.650 --> 01:08:01.890
so that's why I name them all, Morse 1971.

01:08:01.890 --> 01:08:04.170
I want to amplify on her question.

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:05.813
I'm kind of wondering,

01:08:07.330 --> 01:08:12.330
how or if that passion
that we all had in the 1969

01:08:12.390 --> 01:08:14.640
when we came here, still exists?

01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:18.420
And I'm also very troubled,
I mean I feel strongly

01:08:18.420 --> 01:08:21.880
that we should have a feminist movement,

01:08:21.880 --> 01:08:23.790
but I also feel that men,

01:08:23.790 --> 01:08:26.100
and I'm not a man so I
can't make them do this.

01:08:26.100 --> 01:08:28.440
But I would love to see men exploring

01:08:28.440 --> 01:08:29.933
what it means to be a man.

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:34.150
I'm seeing bullying,
I'm seeing intimidating,

01:08:34.150 --> 01:08:36.800
I'm seeing predatory behavior,

01:08:36.800 --> 01:08:38.423
and to me that's not a man.

01:08:39.290 --> 01:08:43.500
I wish men would start to ask if that can

01:08:43.500 --> 01:08:44.883
be something they look at.

01:08:45.740 --> 01:08:47.490
- We had one more hand right there.

01:08:51.010 --> 01:08:54.860
- Yeah, I'm Julia Preston, Class of 1973.

01:08:54.860 --> 01:08:57.573
My question is about women and power.

01:08:58.710 --> 01:09:03.710
Our activism was
anti-establishment in 1969,

01:09:03.980 --> 01:09:07.190
Civil Rights Movement,
the Black Power Movement.

01:09:07.190 --> 01:09:09.560
We were on the outside,

01:09:09.560 --> 01:09:14.030
hurling ourselves against
established power.

01:09:14.030 --> 01:09:17.260
And I feel like, to a certain extent that,

01:09:17.260 --> 01:09:22.260
framed our activism and
our participation for

01:09:22.550 --> 01:09:24.290
throughout our lives.

01:09:24.290 --> 01:09:27.210
And so my question, I have a
question for the congresswoman

01:09:27.210 --> 01:09:30.280
which was, at what moment and why

01:09:30.280 --> 01:09:32.640
did you understand that your role was

01:09:32.640 --> 01:09:35.690
to gain political power?

01:09:35.690 --> 01:09:38.730
And anyone else who wants
to answer that question,

01:09:38.730 --> 01:09:40.740
Nancy Vickers, for example.

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:44.120
How are the women at
Bryn Mawr thinking about

01:09:44.120 --> 01:09:47.110
their relationship with political power?

01:09:47.110 --> 01:09:50.120
How important was it to
have political power?

01:09:50.120 --> 01:09:53.520
Where do we stand out that today?

01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:55.020
- All right maybe let's
start with that question,

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:57.260
and then we'll hear some more reflections

01:09:57.260 --> 01:10:00.020
about men, masculinity.

01:10:00.020 --> 01:10:02.363
But either Nancy Vickers or Congresswoman.

01:10:03.640 --> 01:10:06.310
- You know, there still remain to this day

01:10:06.310 --> 01:10:08.590
many great virtues to
the women's colleges.

01:10:08.590 --> 01:10:13.590
And one of them is that they do tend to be

01:10:13.870 --> 01:10:17.040
very politically engaged.

01:10:17.040 --> 01:10:18.930
They can be still yet more politically,

01:10:18.930 --> 01:10:21.550
mind you I left Bryn Mawr in 2008.

01:10:21.550 --> 01:10:23.363
So a decade has passed.

01:10:24.390 --> 01:10:27.200
As certainly the ethos has changed

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:29.023
under the weight of social media.

01:10:30.080 --> 01:10:34.540
But I certainly do see, as I
listen to the current president

01:10:34.540 --> 01:10:37.530
of Bryn Mawr, someone who is working hard

01:10:37.530 --> 01:10:42.290
with an enormous amount
of forward pressure,

01:10:42.290 --> 01:10:44.430
in terms of making the college

01:10:44.430 --> 01:10:47.713
a politically more responsible
and more active place.

01:10:50.322 --> 01:10:53.230
So consequently I think, you know,

01:10:53.230 --> 01:10:56.020
at least within that cohort
they're graduating strong,

01:10:56.020 --> 01:10:57.210
they have great ambitions,

01:10:57.210 --> 01:10:59.597
and many of those ambitions are political.

01:11:03.230 --> 01:11:05.398
- I thank you for the question.

01:11:05.398 --> 01:11:07.135
I thank you for the question.

01:11:07.135 --> 01:11:10.780
I think it was, there are many

01:11:12.540 --> 01:11:15.160
spheres of influence and power.

01:11:15.160 --> 01:11:20.040
And certainly for a long
time, Yale drove people toward

01:11:20.040 --> 01:11:22.903
the corporate boardroom
and corporate America.

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:28.220
And wound up on Wall Street
and move money around.

01:11:28.220 --> 01:11:31.530
That was one of Yale's pathway

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:33.543
along with Ivy Leagues in general.

01:11:35.632 --> 01:11:38.910
Academics came in and
social policy came in.

01:11:38.910 --> 01:11:43.380
I think in getting out
of Yale and going on

01:11:43.380 --> 01:11:47.560
to law school and beyond, and back home,

01:11:47.560 --> 01:11:49.370
one began to see

01:11:50.220 --> 01:11:55.220
that change came by someone
having money to change

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:56.960
to make a change.

01:11:56.960 --> 01:12:00.080
But it also came by
engagement and changing

01:12:00.080 --> 01:12:01.930
the political construct.

01:12:01.930 --> 01:12:06.490
And so, just to make a difference I think

01:12:06.490 --> 01:12:11.437
was my viewing the political process

01:12:12.780 --> 01:12:14.433
as one to be engaged in.

01:12:16.190 --> 01:12:17.720
Politics comes in many forms.

01:12:17.720 --> 01:12:19.430
It didn't hit me really early on.

01:12:19.430 --> 01:12:23.790
Because as a lawyer, I wanted
to just assume the bench,

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:28.790
and be quiet and proceed on
as something that I loved.

01:12:30.122 --> 01:12:34.550
It was a series of ironic circumstances

01:12:34.550 --> 01:12:37.050
after I relocated.

01:12:37.050 --> 01:12:42.050
The death of Mickey LeLand,
a congressperson who had

01:12:43.710 --> 01:12:48.710
a major focus on helping
the unempowered in Africa,

01:12:49.610 --> 01:12:54.610
and died in Africa trying to provide food

01:12:55.320 --> 01:12:57.680
to starving Ethiopians and Eritreans.

01:12:57.680 --> 01:13:01.000
But in any event that set
my pathway of opportunity

01:13:01.000 --> 01:13:02.940
to becoming elected.

01:13:02.940 --> 01:13:05.610
Just by having to come

01:13:06.650 --> 01:13:09.310
behind that tragedy in our community.

01:13:09.310 --> 01:13:12.020
I just knew that whatever I did,

01:13:12.020 --> 01:13:15.063
I wanted to make sure
someone's life was better.

01:13:16.446 --> 01:13:19.573
And I still see that
in young people today.

01:13:21.640 --> 01:13:24.410
I think what they miss
is having gone through

01:13:24.410 --> 01:13:28.320
what we went through, and
what we know and what we see.

01:13:28.320 --> 01:13:31.293
And I think it's our
obligation to teach them.

01:13:32.540 --> 01:13:35.200
I was framed by my
exposure to Barbara Jordan.

01:13:35.200 --> 01:13:38.590
I was framed by my exposure
to Shirley Chisholm

01:13:38.590 --> 01:13:43.590
and perspective, by Congresswoman
Schroeder out of Colorado.

01:13:44.830 --> 01:13:49.020
I sat next to her as
only one other member,

01:13:49.020 --> 01:13:50.710
there were three of us on
the judiciary committee,

01:13:50.710 --> 01:13:53.160
three women when I came.

01:13:53.160 --> 01:13:54.920
And they told me that I wasn't gonna be

01:13:54.920 --> 01:13:56.430
on any other committee but judiciary,

01:13:56.430 --> 01:13:59.320
because my predecessor,
one of my predecessors,

01:13:59.320 --> 01:14:02.000
Barbara Jordan was on
the judiciary committee.

01:14:02.000 --> 01:14:04.730
We went through what we
call partial birth abortion,

01:14:04.730 --> 01:14:05.973
that's how long I've been.

01:14:05.973 --> 01:14:09.710
That was the attack on
something so outrageous.

01:14:09.710 --> 01:14:12.103
But we as the women, we were called names,

01:14:13.370 --> 01:14:16.260
ridiculous names, because we were the ones

01:14:16.260 --> 01:14:19.820
just going against these men
about how ridiculous it was

01:14:19.820 --> 01:14:23.000
for you to try to outlaw
a medical procedure.

01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:26.690
I'm just using this to say,
that helped gel me even more.

01:14:26.690 --> 01:14:31.310
So I think you do have to
have experiences to say,

01:14:31.310 --> 01:14:35.010
I've got to grab power in some way,

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:37.130
and I've got to be good about that power,

01:14:37.130 --> 01:14:39.590
so that I can do good things.

01:14:39.590 --> 01:14:42.710
And I think anyone in here
should think about that,

01:14:42.710 --> 01:14:46.320
even more now, because I really believe

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:50.160
our going in and out of
feminism, our having it.

01:14:50.160 --> 01:14:54.117
Then moments were people
say, "We're equal."

01:14:55.750 --> 01:14:58.480
I think now more than ever
we need to be teachers,

01:14:58.480 --> 01:15:01.810
as well as actors, and
purveyors of the truth,

01:15:01.810 --> 01:15:03.160
we need to be activists,

01:15:03.160 --> 01:15:05.220
we need to be politically empowered.

01:15:05.220 --> 01:15:08.520
But for me it was, how do I change,

01:15:08.520 --> 01:15:10.430
how do I be a change maker?

01:15:10.430 --> 01:15:13.363
Even as a young mother,
how do I be a change maker?

01:15:14.630 --> 01:15:17.620
We need more people thinking,
how can I be a change maker

01:15:17.620 --> 01:15:22.600
and use political power, but
have your life experience

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:24.040
be part of that political power?

01:15:24.040 --> 01:15:26.253
I think we will better for it as a nation.

01:15:27.760 --> 01:15:28.963
- Can I add something?

01:15:31.800 --> 01:15:34.703
This is not in disagreement. (sighs)

01:15:37.704 --> 01:15:38.770
Julie, I think you said,

01:15:38.770 --> 01:15:41.213
wasn't our passion anti-establishment?

01:15:42.980 --> 01:15:44.473
Anti-establishment.

01:15:45.641 --> 01:15:50.620
- [Julie] (voice faded off
microphone) To protest.

01:15:50.620 --> 01:15:54.320
- Right, I understand
that, but can you think

01:15:54.320 --> 01:15:59.223
of a period of time, including
now, when that is not,

01:16:00.110 --> 01:16:03.480
when there isn't a reason
to be anti-establishment?

01:16:03.480 --> 01:16:06.700
We don't use the terminology,
but I mean really?

01:16:06.700 --> 01:16:09.243
I'm sorry, in this group, really?

01:16:10.660 --> 01:16:12.430
Who's empowering this country?

01:16:13.830 --> 01:16:15.130
(Julie speaks off microphone)

01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:16.720
Yes, I know!

01:16:16.720 --> 01:16:19.780
So why then and not now?

01:16:19.780 --> 01:16:21.750
I haven't figured it out.

01:16:21.750 --> 01:16:23.790
Backlash maybe, but I haven't figured it--

01:16:23.790 --> 01:16:26.280
- I agree. (laughs)
- I agree!

01:16:26.280 --> 01:16:28.070
- Do you want to reflect on some of

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:30.130
the earlier questions as well?

01:16:30.130 --> 01:16:33.150
- Well I'll just say, I
mean there's many ways.

01:16:33.150 --> 01:16:35.603
We all have different ways
we can exercise power.

01:16:36.630 --> 01:16:41.190
Not only in, over politics, but you know

01:16:41.190 --> 01:16:43.120
we live in politics.

01:16:43.120 --> 01:16:47.600
I'll just say, my
daughter is a film maker.

01:16:47.600 --> 01:16:50.230
And she has a big project going.

01:16:50.230 --> 01:16:53.130
And they were supposed to
film in the state of Georgia,

01:16:53.130 --> 01:16:55.660
which has a billion dollar film industry,

01:16:55.660 --> 01:17:00.070
because they give deep tax
breaks to film producers.

01:17:00.070 --> 01:17:04.310
And Georgia passed this
horrible anti-abortion law

01:17:04.310 --> 01:17:07.613
a few months ago, and my
daughter said, "Actually,

01:17:08.579 --> 01:17:11.597
"I'm not taking this 20 million
dollar project to Georgia.

01:17:11.597 --> 01:17:13.130
"I refuse to go there."

01:17:13.130 --> 01:17:15.000
And the whole thing had to pick up

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:17.113
and to go Calgary, Alberta.

01:17:18.230 --> 01:17:21.451
So, that was an exercise of power.

01:17:21.451 --> 01:17:24.451
(audience applauds)

